    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Avicenna wrote his works while moving between the courts of Iranian rulers as physician, administrator and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, at times in hiding or in prison.</li><li>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-canon-of-medicine">Canon of Medicine</a> became a standard medical text in the Islamic world and at many European universities, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> he was ranked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>.</li><li>His philosophy, built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and al-Farabi, distinguished essence from existence and argued for a Necessary Existent identified with God; al-Ghazali and other Islamic scholars condemned parts of it as unbelief.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Of about 450 works Avicenna is believed to have written, around 240 survive, including 150 on philosophy and 40 on medicine, and almost half of his works are in verse.</p></aside>
    </section>

